  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nTonight through Monday...\n\nScattered storms trained over northeast Nebraska this morning, \ndropping a quick 1-3\" of rain, and occasionally flirting with severe \nlimits. A Flood Advisory was issued near Niobrara, where heaviest \nrainfall persisted. Storm coverage will continue to gradually \ndiminish through the late morning, allowing oppressive heat to reign \nover the region. By noon, temperatures had spanned from the lower \n70s, where cloud cover prevailed in northeast Nebraska, to the low \n90s over southeast Nebraska where sunshine was abundant. Dewpoints \nin the low to mid 70s made it feel 100-105 in the hottest locations.\n\nA shortwave disturbance moving out of the Rockies will help ignite \nanother round of storms that will approach from the west late \ntonight into Sunday morning. Storms, fueled by daytime heating, are \nexpected to increase in coverage through the afternoon. Around 20-30 \nkts of deep layer shear could be just enough to help organize a \nstrong storm or two capable of producing damaging winds or \nmarginally severe hail during the late afternoon and evening hours. \nThe greatest chance for severe weather will likely be over far \nnortheast Nebraska into western Iowa. \n\nThe good news is persistent patchy cloud cover combined with a brief \nflattening of the upper ridge should be just enough to take \ntemperatures down a few degrees. Expect afternoon highs to \"only\" \nreach the upper 80s and low 90s, dropping heat indices to 90-100. \nSimilar temperatures are expected Monday, due in part by scattered \nstorm chances persisting, as the shortwave continues to make its way \naround the periphery of the ridge. \n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nA low pressure system that has been churning over the Pacific \nNorthwest this weekend will finally eject across southern Canada by \nmidweek, dragging a cold front through the Central Plains.  The \nexact timing and speed of the passage will dictate shower and storm \npotential, with an afternoon passage leading to higher strong to \nsevere storm potential as storms try to take full advantage of \nsurface instability. \n\nThe boundary will finally push south of the forecast area on \nThursday, with more zonal flow expected to prevail in its wake. \nWhile temperatures may surge into the mid and upper 90s ahead of the \nfront on Tuesday, expect some relative relief from the heat (highs \nin the 80s) to arrive by Wednesday and continue through the end of \nthe week.",
